= Spurious correlation =

== Overview ==
A '''spurious correlation''' is an observed association that does not represent the stable task-relevant relationship one intends to learn and can disappear, reverse, or become misleading under intervention, environmental change, subgroup change, or new sampling.

In machine learning, spurious correlations can support high benchmark accuracy while harming robustness or transfer.

== Causes ==
Spurious associations can arise from:

* confounding;
* selection effects;
* measurement artifacts;
* sampling imbalance;
* background/context features;
* acquisition pipelines;
* temporal coincidence;
* label leakage.

Different mechanisms require different analysis.

== Noncausal does not always mean useless ==
A predictor can be noncausal yet remain reliably predictive under a fixed deployment distribution.

Therefore:

<pre>
spurious correlation ≠ every noncausal correlation
</pre>

“Spurious” is a claim about relevance/stability relative to the task and intended environment, not simply about causality in the abstract.

== Machine learning ==
If a model relies on a spurious feature that is strongly correlated with the target during training, performance can collapse when that correlation changes.

This is a major source of out-of-distribution failure.

== Shortcut learning ==
[[Shortcut learning]] describes the learner's tendency to exploit easy predictive cues.

A spurious correlation is a property of the data/relation; shortcut learning is a pattern of learned behavior.

== Related concepts ==
[[Correlation]], [[Causality]], [[Selection bias]], [[Distribution shift]], [[Shortcut learning]], and [[Generalization (machine learning)]] provide the statistical and learning context.

[[Robustness]] provides the broader perturbation-sensitive performance context in which dependence on unstable correlations becomes visible.

